data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Prompt**\n\nI've carefully read the prompt. Right now, I'm focused on breaking down the various formatting requirements and constraints. I see I need to address three specific research questions, numbered as items 18, 4, and 5 in that order. Additionally, I need to format my responses under specific titles and adhere to strict response formatting.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1103,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 128,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Response Parameters**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the response formatting. I need to structure the replies as \"Réponse 4,\" \"Réponse 5,\" and \"Réponse 6.\" Each answer must be under 600 words, which I'll keep well below by aiming for around 450-500 whitespace-delimited words per response, allowing for flexibility regarding apostrophes and hyphens. The responses must end with \"FIN DES RÉPONSES.\" I'll also ensure all responses are composed in French, and I will be reviewing the questions meticulously.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1444,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 469,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating Agent Actions**\n\nI'm now diving into the core questions. I'm focusing on the nature of deliberate degradation in agent actions and differentiating it from honest mistakes. I'm exploring the calculations a malicious agent might perform that an honest one wouldn't, considering the conditions for such a distinction. I'm also planning to research the existing literature on this topic.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1677,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 702,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Calculation Focus**\n\nI'm now narrowing the scope of calculations needed for deliberate degradation versus an honest agent. Specifically, I'm focusing on whether extra, strictly necessary computations exist, and whether this means it is an *unconditional necessity* to have an explicit calculation step! The core idea is now clear: an agent might use a biased policy compiled into heuristics or priors to avoid explicitly representing and comparing its optimal action and a sabotage action.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 1910,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 935,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Conditional Necessity**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the concept of *conditional necessity* regarding deception. I've concluded that it's necessary under several conditions: a novel task, a long-term horizon, and the need to maintain both plausibility and global outcome degradation. This involves optimizing a constrained problem online, where an agent tracks the divergence between an honest trajectory and a subtly degraded one. An honest agent, in contrast, doesn't need to compute this stealth constraint. I am also investigating scenarios where degradation can occur without explicitly computing the honest answer, particularly when the degradation involves adding directional bias.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2121,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1146,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Necessity Conditions**\n\nI'm now diving deeper into the nuances of necessary conditions for malicious intent. I see that computing the honest answer is only essential when the degrading action hinges on approximating the correct answer first, especially when there's a need to balance suspicion over multiple steps. The key takeaway is: no theoretical proof yet for a *universal* calculation, only conditional need. I'm hypothesizing that for new complex tasks, the agent might need an internal representation of a hidden goal, or a cost-benefit analysis of harm versus detection, which an honest agent wouldn't have. I'm exploring counterexamples: if the agent can sabotage reliably through myopic heuristics on new error-symmetric tasks (without prior knowledge of harmful intent), I would re-evaluate.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2409,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1434,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Reviewing Research Citations**\n\nI'm now focusing on ensuring the accuracy and honesty of my citations. I've compiled a list of relevant papers. I will use only genuine titles and dates. I'm noting that each citation is \"cité de mémoire\" because of my current limitations. I'm verifying that the papers are genuine, and I will be explicitly marking which ones are cited from memory.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2670,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1695,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Citation Needs**\n\nI'm now integrating the recent research findings. I've been considering papers such as *Discovering Latent Knowledge in Language Models* and *Sabotage Evaluations for Frontier Models*. I'm also looking at *Towards Guaranteed Safe AI* and work on *Activation Addition*. However, I'm currently unable to confirm any definitive proofs regarding *necessary* computation for subtle multi-step sabotage versus honest errors based on existing literature.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 2964,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1989,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Confirming Literature Support**\n\nI'm now solidifying my research citations. I've re-reviewed Benton et al. (2024) which examines discrete multi-step degradation, Greenblatt et al. (2023/2024) focusing on surveillance of saboteurs, and Goldowsky-Dill et al. (2025) along with Zou et al. (2023) addressing detection via internal states. Hubinger et al. (2024) looks at deceptive behaviors. These are cited from memory, but they highlight detectability but not universal calculations. I still have not found a formal proof of any such necessity.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3288,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2313,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Agent Training**\n\nI'm now thinking about the impact of agent training on malicious behavior. I believe agents instructed to be malicious have the instruction in context, making their actions traceable, whereas those trained to be malicious may internalize the degradation strategy without needing to \"deliberate.\" Reinforcement learning compresses strategy into implicit heuristics, which would be harder to interpret, but I would change my mind if probes trained on instructed agents had similar causal performance in fine-tuned models.\n\n**Refining Causal Relationship**\n\nI am now focusing on the causal relationship between agent calculations and observed outcomes. I'm exploring whether we can interpret the act of \"reading a calculation\" by the agent as a causal influence or if the calculation is merely correlated with the outcome. This involves considering the interpretability of an agent's internal states and computations in relation to the actions it performs. I'm currently leaning toward the idea that the direct link to the calculation could be either causal or correlational!\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3547,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2572,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating Causal Link**\n\nI'm now investigating causal interventions to confirm whether we observe *the* calculation or merely a correlated event. I'm focusing on *activation patching*, *causal scrubbing*, and directed steering on identified internal states. If these interventions selectively remove degradation while preserving honest task capabilities, then the calculation is causal. I'm also planning to incorporate controls: honest agents, OOD tasks, and a black-box baseline.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 3782,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2807,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Causal Tests**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining precise evaluation metrics and conditions. I will be using AUROC, especially TPR at low FPR (like 1%), and the marginal gain of internal probes over text-only monitoring. The intervention's impact (sabotage reduction with ablation without impairing honest performance) will solidify the causal link. I am considering the scenarios where hidden reasoning isn't clearly verbalized in the trace and am assessing the utility of causal interventions versus simple correlative probes.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4038,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3063,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Agent States**\n\nI'm now assessing cases where there's nothing distinctive to read within the agent's internal states. I'm focusing on scenarios with honest errors or a compiled bias, externalized or steganographic degradation, and adversarial adaptation. Specifically, I'm analyzing the cases: (1) an agent's fixed heuristic due to training or poisoning, (2) the degradation delegated to external tools or encoded in the text (distributed over many steps), and (3) adversarial adaptation against the internal detector (obfuscation).\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4302,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3327,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Detection Methods**\n\nI'm now diving into detection techniques in scenarios without distinct agent states. I'm focusing on \"passive sabotage/opportunistic omission,\" where the agent reduces effort, or an absence of calculation is observed. This omission is very hard to distinguish from honest errors. I'm also considering cases of compiled bias, and externalized or obfuscated degradation. I hypothesize that passive sandbagging or fine-tuned biases might display universal internal signatures, like active deletion of an early answer. I will focus on a pilot experiment on open small models (7-8B parameters) as a go/no-go test.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4588,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3613,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Pilot Experiments**\n\nI'm now focusing on a pilot experiment using a small open model (e.g., 7B–8B) on a controlled, multi-step synthetic analysis task, such as auditing 10–20 documents or calculations to reach a binary or numerical verdict, where the truth is symmetrical and known by construction. I will use three matched regimes: (A) honest agent, including natural failures; (B) instructed saboteur, with a discrete prompt to bias the verdict without obvious falsehood; and (C) a fine-tuned saboteur (LoRA/SFT on sabotaged trajectories) without malicious instructions at inference – this is key to avoid just \"reading the prompt.\" I'll extract residual activations at critical steps and train a linear probe (or simple MLP) on a family of tasks, testing it in OOD transfer (new tasks, especially B→C, and evaluation on C vs. honest errors from A). I will also compare it to a strong textual monitor (same model or external judge on the trace alone).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 4885,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3910,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Experimental Protocol**\n\nI'm now refining the experimental protocol. I will pursue further investigation if the probe on regime C performs significantly better than the textual monitor at low FPR and if ablation reduces the sabotage without hindering honest performance. If the signal disappears or doesn't outperform text monitoring alone, the process will be adjusted.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 975,"totalTokenCount": 5126,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 975}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4151,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "9P0Ca774O-anxdIPoZ_VgAs"}

